nique our  Constitution is nearly as all the sewers  and our democratic institution have have  long been a model to our neighbors this  is the true significance of your current  struggle for the greatest threat to our  democracy is narco-terrorism and in  insatiable worldwide demand for drug  which fuels it in the past you you we  usually saw extremist ideologies as the  most serious threat to democracy but now  drugs and organized crime are even more  dangerous not only to our democracy but  to us as well this is an auspicious  moment to reflect on our changing world  and a new global order look at what has  happened since I graduated from MIT in  1943 a terrible war was raging in Europe  and Asia  taking lives and devastating the  countryside at every turn no one can  ever forget it tragedy of this war the  45 million killed the NASA death camps  the destruction of towns cities of lives  and hopes of generations to come the use  of atomic weapons at Hiroshima and  Nagasaki seven years later when I  returned to to MIT for postgraduate  studies  Europe was still rebuilding the ravages  of this war of the Cold War appeared to  be a reality that will never leave  Soviets and Americans stood poised  ready to strike each other at a moment's  notice  threatening the existence of the human  right race around the world democracy  was in retreat and totalitarian regime  flourished on nearly every continent the  result of the talat Aryan regimes in  Latin America were the same as  everywhere the crushing of the will of  the people the violation of fundamental  human right the demise of democracy  during this cold war the developed world  chose to look the other way as millions  in Latin America suffer under oppression  u.s. foreign policy remain fixated on  east-west competition and north-south  view rarely came into focus and yet in  the last decade Democratic Change has  swept our continent ten years ago most  countries of Latin America suffer under  the weight of dictatorships just look at  the change Peru returned to democracy in  1984 Libya in 1982 and Tina in 1983  Brazil in 1985  Chile Nicaragua Panama in the last year  alone almost a whole continent has moved  from military rule to lay it legitimate  legitimately elected leaders still  evidence of North America's virus to the  Cold War ideology this dramatic change  so close to your borders received  relatively little attention now it's  time to open our eyes to a new world  after years of superpower conflict each  one of you what each one is vying for  good gains in the high-stakes game for  global military advantage we are now  able to see beyond the east-west  confrontation today's global order no  longer rests on the foundation of  security concerns military might is no  longer with the terminus as countries  place in the international system we are  now able to look beyond the myopia of  the Cold War  vision Oh previously clouded horizon to  where a new world is waiting  a world where economic growth and  technological innovation will be of  critical importance not sheer military  strength a were were were entrepreneurs  and innovators will lead the way not the  generals of yesterday witness Japan a  country that spends little on military  but that has suddenly leaped to the  front ranks of were powers it is a  country that gives more economic aid to  the third world than any other nation  including historical in generous United  States dismal military establishment of  Japan presents no serious threat to the  vast armies of the US or Soviet Union  but through its economic might it will  through global influence today which  country I ask do others most want to  emulate today Japan or the Soviet Union  the economic growth through Asia and in  Europe has been extraordinary now we  must work to spend the economic success  of these dynamic regions to other areas  this is particularly true for the  fragile new democracies of  for these countries to solidify their  political gains they will need economic  success from Poland to Argentina to the  Philippines nearly free people expect  economic growth and improved living  standards and in order to maintain  stability this expectation must be met  it is your responsibility and those in  other wealthy countries to ensure the  fostering of global economic growth in  this way you can help these fledgling  democracies as they struggle to spread  their wings this is especially true in  Latin America a region which  historically historically has received  far too little attention the best way to  foster north-south cooperation will not  be through military means but rather by  guaranteeing access of Latin America's  democracies to economic prosperity and  change just as history will recall the  1980s as an era when democratic forces  swept the globe  it will also be remember I said the cade  of resurgence in free-market economics  this is a revolution of enormous  significance a recognition of our global  economy and a confirmation that open  economies with access to markets can  lead to social progress it seems that in  long-running match between Karl Marx and  an Adam Smith is finally coming to an  end  I applaud this development but at the  risk of sounding passe let me issue a  warning in our rise to embrace free  market forces let us not lose sight of  the fundamental role of government and  it embodies the free and collective will  of its people there is a role for both  market forces and we stayed in solving  social problems free-market policies  should not be used as an excuse for the  lack of political will where it is a  sure injustice of fighting our drug  trafficking or providing basic education  and health care free market economics is  not a magic wand which somehow will  relieve us of the obligation to care for  our fellow human beings  no where is the role of the state as  clear as with another global issue which  has been pushed aside for too long the  preservation of our environment this is  a struggle that must be won or highest  priorities in the decade of the 90s I  firmly believe that the industrialized  countries have an ecological debt to  humanity unless in less than two  centuries Industrial Development has not  only destroyed most of the native  forests of Europe and North America but  also has brought pollution acid rain and  destruction to the ozone layer this is  an ecological debt to future generations  who will have to live with the  consequences of our thoughtless ways in  which the developed countries have  achieved the standard of living the  burden of sustaining a viable planetary  environment now rests clearly on the  shoulders of the third world for we are  the last frontier of unspoiled lands the  only way our countries can meet this  challenge is by defeating rural poverty  and economic stagnation the best way for  the United States and other  industrialized countries to pay their  ecological debt to humanity is to be the  partners in this cause my administration  has already set aside more than 14  million acres of rainforest as Indian  reserves in the Amazon region an area  larger larger than these six New England  states together  let us pledge to seek some development  policies which recognize the value of  our most precious resources let us place  to retire this debt now for future  generations  you are you are indeed as fortunate as I  was to be embarking on new careers  during a watershed period the record of  human history is marked by these moments  of fundamental change you now inherit a  new global order with new challenges and  extraordinary opportunities as you  watched the joy of your joint German  colleagues dancing in the streets or the  soul of the faces of daughter brave  young students at kahneman square or the  courage of young Colombian soldiers  fighting for democracy against narco  terrorists it may have been difficult  for you here on this beautiful campus in  Boston to truly appreciate such  democratic furrball here in America  where so much is so often taken for  granted this struggle for democracy may  indeed seem distant however you are all  new graduates one of the world's most  prestigious academic institutions that  honor brings with it a special  responsibility the new democracies  around the world are fragile and need  your help to survive as you look to your  future I ask one thing to the LED  today's flowering democracies wither on  the vine  extend your arms to those who have for  too long lived in oppression in fear and  in poverty like all the newborns  these infant democracies around the  world may at times falter and stumble  hesitantly trying their first steps yet  they must prevail this is our first duty  all of us especially you here in the  United States now have an opportunity to  build in peace what what is often  unavailable by force it is our  collective responsibility to ensure that  this opportunity does not pass us by you  have been well trained I have no doubt  that you have the intellectual power to  face any challenge I harbor the hopes  that you will also have the heart to  meet these challenges with fairness and  compassion I know you will for this  after all is our shared heritage and you  my friends are now the guardians of our  shared vision thank you  you
 

